{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-2287" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for $x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y)=2x.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13992403/state.json" "file_sha256" "4f90318cf65dc238d8a30f0030c21c9600c8a9a43a3e4e2ec0f0f8868b34cc2c"} {"question" "q-2287" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $x,$ assuming that $y=y(x).$\n\nNote that to differentiate the $x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y$ term, we will need to use the product rule.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y) & =2x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y)) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2x) \\\\ [(x)(\\frac{1}{y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{dy}}{\\text{d}x})+(1)(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y))] & =2\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we solve the equation for $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{y}(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y) & =2 \\\\ \\frac{x}{y}(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}) & =2-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{y(2-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y))}{x}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13992403/state.json" "file_sha256" "4f90318cf65dc238d8a30f0030c21c9600c8a9a43a3e4e2ec0f0f8868b34cc2c"} {"question" "q-2287" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13992403/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6cfed199c4140a906832a9f24315991cbcb61b96b2f7063642e453a632b4da06"} {"question" "q-2287" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13992403/state.json" "file_sha256" "4f90318cf65dc238d8a30f0030c21c9600c8a9a43a3e4e2ec0f0f8868b34cc2c"} {"question" "q-2287" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{2-y\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y)}{xy}"] ["math" "\\frac{2y-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y)}{x}"] ["math" "\\frac{x(2-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y))}{y}"] ["math" "\\frac{x(2y-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y))}{y}"] ["math" "\\frac{y(2-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y))}{x}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13992403/state.json" "file_sha256" "4f90318cf65dc238d8a30f0030c21c9600c8a9a43a3e4e2ec0f0f8868b34cc2c"} {"question" "q-2287" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{y(2-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y))}{x}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13992403/state.json" "file_sha256" "4f90318cf65dc238d8a30f0030c21c9600c8a9a43a3e4e2ec0f0f8868b34cc2c"} {"question" "q-14680" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The equation of a curve is $2{x}^{2}+6{y}^{2}=8.$ The tangent line at the point $(1,\\,1)$ on the curve intersects the $y$-axis at the point $P.$ What are the coordinates of $P?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13992403/state.json" "file_sha256" "4f90318cf65dc238d8a30f0030c21c9600c8a9a43a3e4e2ec0f0f8868b34cc2c"} {"question" "q-14680" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First of all, we need to find the slope of the tangent line at the point $(1,\\,1).$  \n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we will calculate the derivative using implicit differentiation, assuming that $y=y(x).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{2}+6{y}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(8) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{2})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(6{y}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ 4x+12y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =0 \\\\ 12y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-4x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{4x}{12y} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{x}{3y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the slope of the tangent at the point $(1,1)$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}_{(1,\\,1)} \\\\  & ={(-\\frac{x}{3y})}_{(1,\\,1)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3\\cdot 1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can write the equation of the tangent at the point $(1,1)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-{y}_{1}) & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ (y-1) & =-\\frac{1}{3}(x-1) \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{3}(x-1)+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we just need to find where this tangent line intersects the $y$-axis. At the point $P$ the $x$-coordinate is $0,$ so we set $x=0$ and solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-\\frac{1}{3}(x-1)+1 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{3}(0-1)+1 \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{3}+1 \\\\ y & =\\frac{4}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of $P$ are $(0,\\,\\frac{4}{3}).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13992403/state.json" "file_sha256" "4f90318cf65dc238d8a30f0030c21c9600c8a9a43a3e4e2ec0f0f8868b34cc2c"} {"question" "q-14680" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13992403/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6cfed199c4140a906832a9f24315991cbcb61b96b2f7063642e453a632b4da06"} {"question" "q-14680" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13992403/state.json" "file_sha256" "4f90318cf65dc238d8a30f0030c21c9600c8a9a43a3e4e2ec0f0f8868b34cc2c"} {"question" "q-14680" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "(0,\\,-\\frac{1}{3})"] ["math" "(0,\\,-\\frac{4}{3})"] ["math" "(0,\\,2)"] ["math" "(0,\\,\\frac{1}{3})"] ["math" "(0,\\,\\frac{4}{3})"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13992403/state.json" "file_sha256" "4f90318cf65dc238d8a30f0030c21c9600c8a9a43a3e4e2ec0f0f8868b34cc2c"} {"question" "q-14680" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "(0,\\,\\frac{4}{3})"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13992403/state.json" "file_sha256" "4f90318cf65dc238d8a30f0030c21c9600c8a9a43a3e4e2ec0f0f8868b34cc2c"} {"question" "q-72214" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for $x{e}^{y}={x}^{2}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13992403/state.json" "file_sha256" "4f90318cf65dc238d8a30f0030c21c9600c8a9a43a3e4e2ec0f0f8868b34cc2c"} {"question" "q-72214" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $x,$ assuming that $y=y(x).$\n\nNote that to differentiate the $x{e}^{y}$ term, we will need to use the product rule.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}x{e}^{y} & ={x}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x{e}^{y}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}) \\\\ [(x)({e}^{y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})+(1)({e}^{y})] & =2x \\\\ x\\,{e}^{y}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{e}^{y} & =2x\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we solve the equation for $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}x{e}^{y}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{e}^{y} & =2x \\\\ x{e}^{y}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2x-{e}^{y} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{2x-{e}^{y}}{x{e}^{y}}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13992403/state.json" "file_sha256" "4f90318cf65dc238d8a30f0030c21c9600c8a9a43a3e4e2ec0f0f8868b34cc2c"} {"question" "q-72214" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13992403/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6cfed199c4140a906832a9f24315991cbcb61b96b2f7063642e453a632b4da06"} {"question" "q-72214" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13992403/state.json" "file_sha256" "4f90318cf65dc238d8a30f0030c21c9600c8a9a43a3e4e2ec0f0f8868b34cc2c"} {"question" "q-72214" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{2x+{e}^{y}}{x\\,{e}^{y}}"] ["math" "\\frac{2x-{e}^{y}}{x\\,{e}^{x}}"] ["math" "\\frac{2x-{e}^{y}}{x\\,{e}^{y}}"] ["math" "\\frac{2x-{e}^{y}}{{e}^{y}}"] ["math" "\\frac{{e}^{y}-2x}{x\\,{e}^{y}}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13992403/state.json" "file_sha256" "4f90318cf65dc238d8a30f0030c21c9600c8a9a43a3e4e2ec0f0f8868b34cc2c"} {"question" "q-72214" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{2x-{e}^{y}}{x\\,{e}^{y}}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13992403/state.json" "file_sha256" "4f90318cf65dc238d8a30f0030c21c9600c8a9a43a3e4e2ec0f0f8868b34cc2c"} {"question" "q-312258" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the slope $m$ of the tangent at the point $(0,\\,0)$ for the curve ${x}^{2}{y}^{3}-4y=6x.$\n\n$m=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13992403/state.json" "file_sha256" "4f90318cf65dc238d8a30f0030c21c9600c8a9a43a3e4e2ec0f0f8868b34cc2c"} {"question" "q-312258" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the slope at a point, we use implicit differentiation to calculate the derivative of the curve, assuming that $y=y(x).$\n\nNote that to differentiate the ${x}^{2}{y}^{3}$ term, we will need to use the product rule.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}{y}^{3}-4y & =6x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}{y}^{3}-4y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(6x) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}{y}^{3})-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(6x) \\\\ [3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2x{y}^{3}]-4\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =6 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2x{y}^{3}-4\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =6 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-4\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =6-2x{y}^{3} \\\\ (3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}-4)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =6-2x{y}^{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{6-2x{y}^{3}}{3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}-4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe slope of the tangent at the point $(0,\\,0)$ is then given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}_{(0,\\,0)} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{6-2x{y}^{3}}{3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}-4})}_{(0,\\,0)} \\\\  & =\\frac{6-2\\cdot 0\\cdot {0}^{3}}{3\\cdot {0}^{2}\\cdot {0}^{2}-4} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{(-4)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13992403/state.json" "file_sha256" "4f90318cf65dc238d8a30f0030c21c9600c8a9a43a3e4e2ec0f0f8868b34cc2c"} {"question" "q-312258" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13992403/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6cfed199c4140a906832a9f24315991cbcb61b96b2f7063642e453a632b4da06"} {"question" "q-312258" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13992403/state.json" "file_sha256" "4f90318cf65dc238d8a30f0030c21c9600c8a9a43a3e4e2ec0f0f8868b34cc2c"} {"question" "q-312258" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{3}{2}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13992403/state.json" "file_sha256" "4f90318cf65dc238d8a30f0030c21c9600c8a9a43a3e4e2ec0f0f8868b34cc2c"} {"question" "q-342401" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13992403/assets/0c89b1d28ce7692f54e0fbf32c1d99b091e24729c837883523d6150450decb17.png)\n\nThe equation $4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=20x$ represents an ellipse. The tangent line at the point $(4,4)$ on the ellipse intersects the $y$-axis at the point $P.$ What are the coordinates of $P?$\n\nThe coordinates of $P$ are $(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13992403/state.json" "file_sha256" "4f90318cf65dc238d8a30f0030c21c9600c8a9a43a3e4e2ec0f0f8868b34cc2c"} {"question" "q-342401" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First of all, we need to find the slope of the tangent line at the point $(4,4).$\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we will calculate the derivative using implicit differentiation, assuming that $y=y(x).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(20x) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4{x}^{2})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({y}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(20x) \\\\ 8x+2y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =20 \\\\ 2y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =20-8x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{20-8x}{2y} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{10-4x}{y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the slope of the tangent at the point $(4,4)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(4,\\,4)} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{10-4x}{y})|}_{(4,\\,4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{10-4\\cdot 4}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-6)}{4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can write the equation of the tangent at the point $(4,4)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-4 & =-\\frac{3}{2}(x-4) \\\\ y & =-\\frac{3}{2}(x-4)+4 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{3}{2}x+10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we just need to find where this tangent line intersects the $y$-axis. At the point $P$ the $x$-coordinate is $0,$ so we set $x=0$ and solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-\\frac{3}{2}x+10 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot 0+10 \\\\ y & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of $P$ are $(0,\\,10).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13992403/state.json" "file_sha256" "4f90318cf65dc238d8a30f0030c21c9600c8a9a43a3e4e2ec0f0f8868b34cc2c"} {"question" "q-342401" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13992403/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6cfed199c4140a906832a9f24315991cbcb61b96b2f7063642e453a632b4da06"} {"question" "q-342401" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13992403/state.json" "file_sha256" "4f90318cf65dc238d8a30f0030c21c9600c8a9a43a3e4e2ec0f0f8868b34cc2c"} {"question" "q-342401" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0"] "categor